r set of common values, and is denoted by the intersection symbol $\\cap.$\n\nFor example, the intersection of the two intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$ is denoted\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nand corresponds to the set of values that are in both intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty).$\n\nTo represent the intersection on a number line, we can start by sketching the two intervals separately:\n\n![](images/a0/a0b486ac0f981fc5a0161c5eabba9ec257bd0a03e5f0e27397f6fe8e81eb39a5.png)\n\n![](images/8a/8a98f83055b49c14ed08fff658ace9527c5134879a66245aff02e446d44a5c87.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a5126928d43be82d3e4a167b52b34d8ed4ec33e94b991ecb8486caf3e3d4a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the intervals is ${\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-\\infty,2]} \\cap {\\color{red}[-1,\\infty)} = {\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1065 8683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db563ec9-015b-505d-a764-ade8484216bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065436 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186567143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186567147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186567148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1045 "Intersections of Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186567143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566351 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "08961f5b-f314-