10 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567010 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the inequalities $-1<x<4$ and $x>2$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03c05eb1-a1b5-5135-a57f-9c96b45d2734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567010 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/f9/f9409f298a0f4d1fd06bdac159e8fd7eb034ee577d2c8956730f0cc3919e051a.png)\n\n![](images/f4/f4fd2c61f600408511d967690cf02c95a3d2fa470aa5d7e9176a222d16823d5b.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/fd/fdf3410c69cf8559abfb7b223fc175bd1704d6334c8abbceb45240c894514172.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567035 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the intersection of intervals $(-2,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty ).$ Express the result as an inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41aba970-483d-56bd-ae8a-765c80a69d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567035 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(1,\\infty )$ separately:\n\n![](images/4b/4bd70e5f89643a5d2b19954003435b4c6aeec24c68bb3cd1f6a4e7a98c14f847.png)\n\n![](images/ef/eff58d0bb2d496d608ae1e415f38589c5b9b13efe1f282d172fb5313ba6792ff.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-2,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/61/6131e16f37e8ec44eae8c085be60a28dd094390e1975bdafe256cdc9e229f3dc.png)\n\nThus, $(-2,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the inequality $x>1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the intersection of intervals $(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (2,4).$ Express the result as an inequality.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a7beadf-3b71-5ed6-8cee-74b01fe2145a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567054 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty ,4]$ and $(2,4)$ separately:\n\n![](images/6e/6ea8f23ba164e820b4ae33f6dbd7e5d70e9dd0b5af052a7ce5ecdcb680d3a895.png)\n\n![](images/81/81f94d814f98021781460cbe7c30470e3021832b40525da36563bb11af9804ad.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (2,4)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/77/772ae637383243d60d40f9bc015c5347b92159b7eccb27cefaa661341176b9d6.png)\n\nThus, $(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (2,4)=(2,4).$ This corresponds to the inequality $2<x<4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567143 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186567147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567143 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc861a89-3878-5857-a936-629ce6d04a38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567143 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186567035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567147 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186567148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567147 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7625faa-1053-58cf-a1fe-1a9cb0cbd666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567147 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186567010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567148 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c801bfca-4ee3-51d3-bf92-d0fa094146e1" #edb/re